ssible. ;-)\n\nIf the relation you're basing your search on is not applicable to the index, you can't make any use of it. Which in return not only means it's slow, but that it's the worst performing search possible: Just grep everything that's there, try to convert it and apply your comparison if conversion was successful.\n\n\nAnother story would be to apply an additional filter afterwards, of course. If the actual query can be articulated without the notion of those types and there relationships, then you can have a \"syntax\" to further restrict the results. If that is what you are asking for, then it's just a matter of \"Is it worth the effort?\", obviously.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1570266438,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzODYzMjEyOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2740#issuecomment-538632129"},"message":"Closing \"question\"-type issue with a response, and no further activity.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1570266438,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MjY4OTE4NTY0Ng=="},"status":2}]}